  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nMulti-model consensus shows that upper level troughing will \nremain over Northern New England through at least early Monday.\nThis will mean mostly cloudy skies for much of the area with\nseasonably chilly temperatures. As an embedded vorticity\nmaximum moves southward through the broader cyclonic flow, \nforcing for ascent will be sufficient for scattered to perhaps \neven a period of widespread snow showers in the mountains. Lower\nelevations likely experience scattered rain showers and maybe \nsome graupel? \n\nThe trough begins to lose its grip on our region during the day\non Sunday. It will still be mostly cloudy and cool, but showery\nweather should be relegated to the mountains.\n\nRidging then starts to nudge in to begin the work week, which \nwill lead to a gradual warming trend back to above normal \ntemperatures. On Monday, highs will mainly range from the upper \n30s to upper 40s, but we will be back to widespread highs in the\nupper 40s to upper 50s by midweek with a few lower 60s possible\nacross the south. With high pressure dominating, next week \nshould remain dry. The next trough may approach at the end of \nthe week into next weekend but nothing looks impactful at the \nmoment.",
